# Performance

Targets: once every tree is applied, a frame that only compares a dozen effect values with
the ones last applied (the sway is the shader's, the falling leaves are Unity's particle
systems), a scan every 2 s (`SceneScanSeconds`), an edit re-applies one tree, 0 B/frame. The plugin shows no timings of its own; measure
with Unity's profiler, or VaM's frame timings, against the scene without the plugin.

- Zero allocation per frame. The tree editor's callbacks and that per-frame comparison set
  dirty flags (the effects' storables carry no callbacks); the tree pass does the work, for
  dirty trees only. Each guarded step's delegate is built once in `Lifecycle`, never at the
  call site.
- An edit in the tree editor touches one tree (`Lifecycle.Touch`); every tree is applied
  again only for a change in an effect's values or MSAA, the leaf shader arriving, the
  master or the plugin switched back on, an effect's reset or a scene restore (both
  `TouchAll`), or Rescan.
- Re-applying a tree writes material properties; a copy is made or dropped only when a
  material's kind changes.
- Leaf shapes are extracted once per source texture, as a coroutine that yields between
  steps, shared by every material on it; the texture is read at most `AtlasReadSize` on a
  side. Never per frame, never per material.
- The scan walks the atom list and checks precedence; a tree's renderers are read only
  when they are stale (`Tree.NeedsGather`), not every time it is dirty.
- Build UI strings only when the page is visible and the text changed, on the slow tick
  (`UiRefreshSeconds`).
